  <h2>Four Effect Groups at a Glance</h2>

  <p>
    The effect picker is organised into four groups. The number in the list header always shows how many effects are currently available.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>GPU Presets:</strong> The largest block. These effects are computed as shaders on the graphics card and stay smooth even in demanding scenes. They cover spectrum, geometry, light, laser, LED, disco, fireworks and aurora.</li>
    <li><strong>Portrait (image):</strong> Five effects that take one of your own images, such as a photo or logo, and distort, glitch or dissolve it into LED dots in time with the music.</li>
    <li><strong>Classic (Canvas2D):</strong> 47 older effects that work without WebGL. They serve as a fallback on devices without WebGL2 and keep existing presets working.</li>
    <li><strong>My Presets:</strong> As soon as you save a layer combination, it shows up here as its own entry.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Most Important Effect Families</h2>

  <h3>Spectrum and Waveform</h3>

  <p>
    The foundation of every visualization: "Bars", "Bars mirrored" and "Radial Bars" show the frequency spectrum as columns, in a circle or mirrored around the centre line. "Waveform" draws the real time-domain signal as a glowing line with ghost traces, "Strings" makes lines vibrate like tuned strings, and "LED Level Meter" recreates the familiar green-yellow-red chain with peak hold. "Audio Landscape" rolls the spectrum history towards the viewer as a mountain range where bass raises the peaks.
  </p>

  <h3>Rings, Cores and Pulses</h3>

  <figure class="blog-demo">
    <img
      src="/image/visualizer-blog-1.png"
      alt="Audio visualizer effect with concentric jagged rings in blue and violet around a brightly glowing white core"
      width="1920"
