    <figcaption>Kaleidoscopic symmetry: "Mandala", "Fractal Bloom" and "Flower of Life" breathe with the bass, the rings light up outward with the spectrum.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    "Mandala" warps noise kaleidoscopically and places spectrum rings around a bass core. "Fractal Bloom" folds a fractal whose distances breathe with the bass and whose rotation follows the onsets. "Flower of Life" is a circle lattice that lights up from the inside out. "Cymatics" shows Chladni figures of a vibrating plate, "Interference" lets orbiting wave sources form moiré patterns, "Crystal Cells" lights a Voronoi mesh at the edges with treble and in the cores with bass, and "Neon Maze" draws endless curves from Truchet tiles that flip on every onset.
  </p>

  <h3>Nature, Fire and Fireworks</h3>

  <p>
    Six aurora effects, from "Northern Lights Curtain" through "Northern Lights Lake" to "Aurora Swirl", bring calm colour veils for ambient and chill-out. "Fire" sends flames up from the bottom that roar on bass and throw embers on treble. Five fireworks variants, including peony, willow, rings, fountains and a "Grand Finale" with a sky flash, launch a rocket on every onset. "Lightning" strikes from the centre to the edge on beats, "Matrix Rain" lets columns of glyphs fall.
  </p>

  <h3>LED Walls, Digits and Letters</h3>

  <p>
    If you want the look of real LED hardware, there are "LED Wall" (3×3 and 5×5), "LED Matrix", "LED Ring", "LED Strips", "LED Blinder", "LED Sunstrip" and "LED Hex Panel". Especially for countdowns and intros: "LED Digit 0" to "LED Digit 9" and "LED Letter A" to "E" form a 5×7 dot matrix where every single lamp has its own frequency band, bass at the bottom, treble at the top.
  </p>

  <h3>Portrait Effects for Your Own Images</h3>

  <p>
    Five effects take an image from the canvas or the gallery and turn it into the visualizer itself: "LED Portrait" rasterises the image into round LEDs, "Glitch Portrait" tears it apart with RGB split, displaced rows and scanlines, "Sound-Wave Portrait" bulges it with the bass and ripples it with the treble, "Contour Portrait" shows only glowing edges, and "Halftone Portrait" prints it as dots that grow with the bass. Ideal for cover art, logos and artist portraits.
  </p>

  <h2>Multi-Layer: Stacking Effects</h2>

  <p>
    A single effect is just the beginning. In multi-layer mode you stack several effects as layers. Each layer has its own settings:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Blend mode:</strong> 16 modes such as Normal, Multiply, Screen, Overlay, Lighten, Difference or Luminosity decide how the layer merges with the layers beneath it.</li>
    <li><strong>Opacity and colour transparency:</strong> Adjustable separately, so an effect works as a subtle background or a strong foreground.</li>
    <li><strong>Position and size:</strong> Every layer can be moved freely and scaled from 10% to 300%. Scaled-down effects automatically get soft edges.</li>
    <li><strong>Own colour and reaction source:</strong> One layer can listen to the bass, the next one to the treble.</li>
    <li><strong>Layer management:</strong> Duplicate, show and hide, reorder, and save as your own preset.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The built-in templates such as "Laser Club", "Disco Night" or "Aurora Sky" show what this looks like: they combine three layers with different blend modes and reaction sources into a finished scene that you can use as a starting point.
  </p>

  <h2>Beat Reactions: What Makes the Effects Dance</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reaction source:</strong> For each effect or layer you choose what it reacts to: spectrum, bass, mids, treble, volume or an onset. Onset sources only jump on beats and settle in between, instead of staying maxed out on continuously loud music. Reaction strength is adjustable from 0 to 100.</li>
    <li><strong>Beat punch:</strong> An onset zoom of the whole layer that reacts to beats rather than sustained loudness. With the "Variation" slider, regions of the layer react to each beat with their own strength and a slight offset, so the image feels more alive than uniform pumping.</li>
    <li><strong>Onset flourishes:</strong> Beat-triggered extras in selected visualizers, such as particle burst, bloom pop, grid punch or orb pop. They are auto-normalised and fire equally on quiet and loud tracks.</li>
    <li><strong>Beat-drop effects:</strong> Full-frame reactions like Flash, Color Burst, Strobe and Vignette Pulse, each with source, intensity and decay.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-processing:</strong> "Bloom / Glow" with strength, threshold and radius, "Motion trails" with adjustable length, and "Adaptive quality" that keeps the frame rate stable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step by Step: Building an Effect Scene</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Open the Audio Visualizer</strong>
        <p>Go to <a href="https://kodinitools.com/visualizer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kodinitools.com/visualizer</a> — free, no sign-up. Load an audio file via drag and drop or choose the microphone as the source.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Choose a format</strong>
        <p>Set the target format in the canvas controls: 9:16 for TikTok, Reels and Shorts, 1:1 for Instagram posts, 16:9 for YouTube, or a free size.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Pick a base effect</strong>
        <p>In the "Visualizer Type" panel click a GPU effect, for example "Light Rays" or "Mandala". Adjust colour and opacity; the preview reacts immediately to the music that is playing.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Add layers</strong>
        <p>Use "Multi-Layer" to create a second layer, for example "Bars" with blend mode "Screen" and reaction source "Bass onset". Set position and size so that both effects complement each other.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Fine-tune the beat reactions</strong>
        <p>In the "Effects (Post-Processing)" popover enable beat punch, set strength and variation, and add bloom and motion trails if you like.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Record and export</strong>
        <p>In the recorder choose 30 or 60 fps, click "Prepare" and "Start", play the track and stop. The video is saved as WebM; optionally it is converted to MP4 or rendered frame by frame at the highest quality via HQ frame export.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>Tip for social media clips:</strong> Combine a calm background effect such as "Aurora" or "Liquid Light" at low opacity with a hard beat effect such as "Centrifugal Rings" or "Fireworks" in the foreground, using the reaction source "Bass onset". The background carries the mood, the foreground makes the beat visible. With beat markers you can additionally switch the effect, colour or background automatically at the drop, with a smooth crossfade if you want.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>More Than Effects: Text, Images, Ticker</h2>

  <p>
    Effects are just one layer of the canvas. On top of them come text elements with typewriter, fade, scale and slide animations and over 30 audio-reactive text effects, a scrolling ticker band that pulses or changes colour to the beat, your own images and videos with filters and beat reactions, a tiled background with up to twelve tiles, plus gradients, background images and background videos. Everything can be placed freely on the canvas.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy: Privacy First</h2>

  <p>
    Audio analysis, effect rendering and recording run entirely locally in your browser via the Web Audio API, Canvas and WebGL. Your audio file is not uploaded. Only the optional server-side exports, namely MP4 conversion, GIF export and HQ frame export, send the finished video or the rendered frames to the KodiniTools server, where the files are deleted automatically after one hour. If you prefer not to, use the direct WebM download and stay completely in the browser.
  </p>

